#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494011} :activity/id #uuid "aa14eb4a-61b9-5764-91b0-789a38c1720c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048973 :topic/id #uuid "d050a7cc-fb5e-58e5-a1a7-41ca68d4da28" :topic/math-academy-id 3574} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493981 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05c441ed-0435-5e2d-83a0-618a3112fcba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348499} {:answer/id #uuid "45842989-10cc-5c73-ad09-63becc6e2b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349079} {:answer/id #uuid "4662c9e9-26e1-5ad9-933f-23f51efda70c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349088} {:answer/id #uuid "5d944e2a-38b8-5891-a36e-9e4f87137e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349308} {:answer/id #uuid "806a25dd-fc2a-5451-8d21-5511e240733a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349624}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349624} :answer-field/id #uuid "390a9ffd-1e61-5aac-8fcf-72786b0b334b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "931d7f2e-4fc8-5dd3-95f9-e9f514cf8458" :question/math-academy-id "q-81184" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{5}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =\\sqrt{5}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-3}{2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-3(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})}{9}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}.$"} :step/id #uuid "156e434a-b38e-5003-b3b2-3990ebd3f421"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494011 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad718acf-bc34-5af5-ab86-3a5dc856b0ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366067} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5c5ebb-e7a9-57dc-ac9a-df4eb523b425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366068} {:answer/id #uuid "7a336dcb-e67a-58d8-bb13-be3f3425f6fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366069} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc1d8b9-1b3b-58d6-9550-65d587e7b93b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366070} {:answer/id #uuid "53304843-707b-558e-bb98-11a42cfed664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366071}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366068} :answer-field/id #uuid "c472b1f5-b749-54e2-afc1-cb077c5b2380" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "597496aa-43f1-55f6-97dc-9702fffad14f" :question/math-academy-id "q-38478" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-2\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(-2-2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-2\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector of ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(-2)(-2)}{(1-\\text{i})(1+\\text{i})}=2.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "64c77246-92ab-5343-a010-84041f103bc9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494042}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494015 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27933876-5b2f-59dc-a5a5-942ee704758c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =2+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493454} {:answer/id #uuid "4695373b-5cc8-56e0-bcf9-e8857baa20e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =4+2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493510} {:answer/id #uuid "7e689100-10cd-5e89-9858-aae9672b630a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =4-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493587} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3dfe99-6eeb-5edd-b06f-b616e7766a94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =2-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493635} {:answer/id #uuid "af381ec0-91d6-5997-9ce5-2412f090232d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493671}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493587} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0cfc4d0-a713-5f9b-8684-1ec443f7a134" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6b62f170-a433-5de2-8209-ac0435d5fb1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81148" :question/problem "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4-8\\text{i} \\\\ 4-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{\\text{i}}^{2}-8\\text{i} \\\\ 4-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i}(4-2\\text{i}) \\\\ 1(4-2\\text{i})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(4-2\\text{i})[\\begin{aligned}-2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(4-2\\text{i})v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=(4-2\\text{i})v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4-2\\text{i}.$"} :step/id #uuid "7665ea0d-01a8-57f6-bbbc-8dc06d46bcb0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493981}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494019 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e6770c-7bf9-5f46-9062-0e30bf90a42d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348592} {:answer/id #uuid "6a72311c-58cb-5b33-9b66-4446eb52c4ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349429} {:answer/id #uuid "b4bf0977-5fa5-50f0-be87-c64eb8d03d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350045} {:answer/id #uuid "c7687cf8-d801-5c98-be83-eb0c3229d84f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350202} {:answer/id #uuid "cd5e115e-8718-54b9-9627-bd37a0f17f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350252}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350252} :answer-field/id #uuid "36e4857a-4c14-5c14-8928-81cdfeac6383" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7207a8c6-6c0f-53ed-83a0-48cf577c32a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-81027" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2+\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3-\\text{i} & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(-3-\\text{i}){x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{5}(3-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3-\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-5}{3-\\text{i}}=\\frac{-5(3+\\text{i})}{10}=-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}.$"} :step/id #uuid "7bf20b01-76bc-543c-a0e8-b3bf2f4563c9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494015}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494042 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2373d10f-9177-5caa-aa21-81933675524a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324263} {:answer/id #uuid "2d0c4ffc-7cb8-5890-8b26-5fc5ba15fdf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324333} {:answer/id #uuid "87d22551-71cd-562b-a2fb-e2c71dff8be4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325128} {:answer/id #uuid "991ed941-102e-554e-9c2b-837d919cb7c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325274} {:answer/id #uuid "f8014819-4600-55cf-996f-8ea4d1e77dc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326154}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325274} :answer-field/id #uuid "afc9f365-fbcc-5a5c-bd09-b46393c41e88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d55b44c8-8d42-5261-b22a-10b38f009304" :question/math-academy-id "q-38494" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-3\\text{i} & 2 \\\\ -5 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=-2-3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(1-3\\text{i})(1+3\\text{i})}{(5)(5)}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9b37de30-2d0f-54d1-9978-97a780796cdb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494019}}] :activity/title "Finding Complex Eigenvectors of Real 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493385}]}
